hello my name is Mary similar kerr i'm  pleased to come and speak with you today  about chronic illness and how to  overcome it in my life I've experienced  both the good and the bad of chronic  illness and I have severe lung disease  now pulmonary fibrosis but all of my  life I was faced with the challenge of  very severe asthma from childhood on and  it just my lungs have just gotten  progressively worse over time in 2007 I  was diagnosed with pulmonary fibrosis  and after exhausting all the medications  and oxygen and everything I could with  my normal phone the pulmonologist I went  for an evaluation at johns hopkins  university medical center and that's  where i was diagnosed and at that time i  was told that the average pulmonary  fiber on occasion lives five to seven  years post diagnosis and i'm now ten  years into that diagnosis oh you can see  that I've made more progress than my  physicians expected me to but whenever I  get really bad news I am a realist and  I'm an optimist at the same time in that  I don't take bad news lightly however I  also don't take it in a fatalistic way I  want to overcome it and so I'm always  looking for that new challenge in my  personal life at the same time which is  like sort of the unfair you know when it  rains it pours story Isaac began to  experience what I like to call my own  personal reign of terror and I lost the  people eight people closest to me in my  life in an eight-year period of time  while at the same time the lung disease  was getting worse and so it was just a  double whammy and I got so depressed at  one point because of complicated grief  that I came to the point of thinking  about suicide and actually apparently  having a psychotic break with reality my  neighbors heard me screaming one night  and they called the ambulance and that's  how I woke up in a psych ward in a  straitjacket strapped to a bed  and when I woke up in this all-white  room I had absolutely no idea where I  was and I had been there for nine days  as I later found out this was really an  eye-opener for me I for the eighth for  the probably five years prior to that I  knew that I was seriously ill with not  only my lung disease but also in my  psyche I was seeing on maybe on monday  wednesday and friday i'd see my  psychiatrist and on tuesdays and  thursdays and sometimes saturday in the  ER as the pulmonologist that's too many  doctors in one's life and so i had to  find ways to cope with it and I really I  really came to that finding it out in a  really stark way in the site in the  hospital in the psychiatric treatment I  was fortunate enough to have a wonderful  psychiatrist and he came up with two  very creative ways for me to overcome my  what I we now know is an existential  vacuum I had no idea what I was going to  do with myself how was going to get  better where I was going to live but I  knew that I couldn't keep staying in my  home where there was no but nothing but  pictures of dead people on my walls and  nothing but oxygen tanks and medical  equipment set up I had to plan a new  life when i said earlier that i had both  the good experience with the good and  the bad about chronic illness the good  news about this having chronic illness  and being forced to stop and what you're  doing get off that fast train of i have  to make more money you have to build a  bigger business i have to do this i have  to do that and coming and being stopped  in your dead dead in your tracks and  then really understanding at the pit of  your soul what you need to do and what's  really important in life and it's  certainly not making money in building  bigger businesses it's being healthy  finding new people to love after you've  after loss and so he came up with two  creative ways for me to plan a new life  for myself he said why don't you think  since you're good at business why don't  you create a business plan for Mary to  point o make it like a real company with  those great board of directors and  that's and I said well no that's  something I can really wrap my head  around because I love to  to create and develop incubate  businesses and the second thing he said  is I'd like for you to read this book  man's search for meaning and I laughed I  miss laughed in his face because I said  I know all about Franco I read his book  in college I met him I know all about  his work and he said well humor me read  it again because it never hurts to read  it again and once i had done both of  those things I really understood  suffering at its core and and I  understood dr. Frankel's work in a way  that I hope most people don't have to  find out firsthand having experienced  the trauma that I experience and having  lived it and living with experience of  the trauma of my own chronic illness I  was forced to understand dr. Frankel's  work at a deeper level so that I could  uncover new meaning in my own life and  he was very very smart I mean obviously  one of the great geniuses of the world  and he taught us three ways that we can  create uncover meaning in our lives  first we can find a way to create  something will I do that with businesses  producing movies producing digital  content I always and create I'm always  creative the second way we can find that  is to experience someone to find a  person to love I've done that I knew  again and the third way we can do it is  to change our attitude towards suffering  or toward any circumstance and and you  don't have to suffer to find meaning but  there is meaning and suffering and that  I learned first hand and so I utilized  wrinkles work in a in both the a  thoughtful way and a meaningful way to  uncover meaning and and I learned that I  could cope and I could get up every  single day and I still have to do that I  have to get up every single day and  realize that I'm not the person I was at  age 35 or 40 who wasn't sick and I have  to  realign and adjust my new reality every  single day okay today you're breathing  pretty good but you're still going to  have to spend two hours Mary taking  medication before you can start your  work day so I get up a little bit  earlier and I plan for whatever the  circumstance is going to be for example  if I know that I'm going out to dinner  that night I don't eat the whole day  because when they eat it lowers it makes  my diaphragm go up and because it  squeezes up the belly and then it it  shortens my vital capacity I don't have  as much air and I can't breathe so I  waited till the latest possible moment  in the day to even eat a meal and those  kind of things you do I had to bring my  office home because it was too much it  drained too much of my energy to go to  the office every day so I brought my  office home and so I work from home now  and I created I found created a new  business film company so I can be a  producer and work from my own computer  in my house so for the most part so what  I would advise anyone who's overcoming  chronic illness and facing a possible  terminal illness is adjust your attitude  in every moment moment by moment minute  by minute day by day year by year and  adjust to that new reality and realize  that you will find is dr. Frankel taught  us meaningful moments every step of the  way I always tell students when I'm  talking about this that they can also  adapt my personal mantra and that is if  you live your life from the inside out  and not from the outside in most of your  day is going to be wonderful anyway  thank you for listening
 

